Q: How to get local time c++ A: <blockquote>
<p>I need to get the current time (hour,minutes and seconds) and add 30 minutes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>From a C point-of-view, which works in C++, add 30 minutes to the <code>.tm_min</code> member  and call <code>mktime()</code> to re-align members.<br />
Robust code checks for errors.</p>
<pre><code>time_t now_plus_minutes(struct tm *localTime, int minutes) {
  time_t currentTime;

  // Get the current time.
  if (time(&amp;currentTime) == -1) {
    return -1;
  }

  // Convert to local time.
  struct tm *localTime = localtime( &amp;currentTime );
  if (localTime == NULL) {
    return -1;
  }

  // Add offset.
  tm-&gt;tm_min += minutes;

  // Re-align struct tm members.
  time_t new_time = mktime(&amp;tm);
  if (new_time == -1) {
    return -1;
  }

  return new_time;
}

int main() {
  struct tm localTime;
  int delta = 30; // minutes
  time_t newTime now_plus_minutes(&amp;localTime, delta);
  if (newTime == -1) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Fail&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;;
  } else {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Execute at: &quot; &lt;&lt; newTime.tm_hour &lt;&lt; &quot;:&quot; &lt;&lt; 
        newTime.tm_min &lt;&lt; &quot;:&quot; &lt;&lt; newTime.tm_sec &lt;&lt; std::endl;
  }
  return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to implement safe Multi-Sig ownership transition in a Solidity ERC-20 token without bricking cross-chain bridge logic? A: <p>The safest way to transition ownership to a Multi-Sig architecture without breaking active bridge operations is to implement a **two-step transfer pattern** (using OpenZeppelin's `Ownable2Step` instead of a basic `Ownable`).</p>
<p>A direct one-step transfer (`transferOwnership`) poses a massive risk: if you accidentally pass an incorrect or improperly formatted Multi-Sig address, the contract becomes permanently bricked with no way to recover.</p>
<p>### Recommended Solution Architecture:</p>
<p>1. **Inherit Ownable2Step:** This ensures that ownership is not transferred immediately. The current owner proposes the Multi-Sig, but the Multi-Sig must explicitly call `acceptOwnership()` to finalize the state change.</p>
<p>2. **Validator Separation:** Keep bridge validation roles (`MINTER_ROLE` or execution vectors) completely distinct from contract configuration controls (`DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE` or `Owner`).</p>
<p>Here is a secure implementation layout using Solidity `^0.8.20`:</p>
<p>```solidity</p>
<p>// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT</p>
<p>pragma solidity ^0.8.20;</p>
<p>import &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol&quot;;</p>
<p>import &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable2Step.sol&quot;;</p>
<p>contract WrappedGramBridgeToken is ERC20, Ownable2Step {</p>
<pre><code>// Explicit tracking for bridge contracts

address public bridgeExecutionTarget;

event BridgeTargetUpdated(address indexed oldTarget, address indexed newTarget);

constructor(string memory name, string memory symbol) 

    ERC20(name, symbol) 

    Ownable(msg.sender) 

{}

modifier onlyBridge() {

    require(msg.sender == bridgeExecutionTarget, &quot;BridgeToken: Caller is not the authorized bridge&quot;);

    \_;

}

function updateBridgeTarget(address \_newTarget) external onlyOwner {

    require(\_newTarget != address(0), &quot;BridgeToken: New target cannot be zero address&quot;);

    emit BridgeTargetUpdated(bridgeExecutionTarget, \_newTarget);

    bridgeExecutionTarget = \_newTarget;

}

function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external onlyBridge {

    \_mint(to, amount);

}

function burn(address from, uint256 amount) external onlyBridge {

    \_burn(from, amount);

}
</code></pre>
<p>}</p>
<p>```</p>
<p>### Why this prevents state corruption during the transition:</p>
<p>* **Zero-Address Protection:** The `Ownable2Step` contract inherently blocks accidental transfers to `address(0)`.</p>
<p>* **Zero Downtime:** Because the bridge validation logic uses the immutable/state-tracked `bridgeExecutionTarget` rather than the `owner` modifier directly, infrastructure operations can process asset bridges continuously while the Multi-Sig signing structure finishes its multi-signature confirmation transactions to assume core administrative control.</p>

Q: Saving Data from Laravel Form in Bootstrap Modal A: <p>They are quiet a few problem i noticed on your code some of which have already been answered by the past contributors, but here is mine</p>
<p>1. The problem is a structural issue with your modal HTML, the <code>&lt;/form&gt;</code> tag is misplaced, which causes the submit button to fall <strong>outside</strong> the form entirely.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;modal-body&quot;&gt;
    {{ Form::open(...) }}   &lt;!-- form opens here --&gt;
    ...fields...
    &lt;/form&gt;                 &lt;!-- form closes here (TOO EARLY) --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;modal-footer&quot;&gt;
    {{ Form::submit(...) }} &lt;!-- submit button is OUTSIDE the form! --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
{{ Form::close() }} 
</code></pre>
<p>Because the submit button is outside the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element, clicking it does nothing — no POST is ever sent.</p>
<p><strong>Suggestion:</strong> Move <code>Form::open()</code> and <code>Form::close()</code> so they wrap the <strong>entire modal content</strong>, including the footer:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div id=&quot;addPenggunaModal&quot; class=&quot;modal hide fade&quot; tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; role=&quot;dialog&quot;&gt;

  {{ Form::open(array('url'=&gt;'users/addpengguna','class'=&gt;'form-horizontal','method'=&gt;'POST')) }}

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-header&quot;&gt;
      &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; class=&quot;close&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;×&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;h3&gt;Tambah Pengguna Baru&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-body&quot;&gt;
      &lt;ul&gt;
          @foreach($errors-&gt;all() as $error)
              &lt;li&gt;{{ $error }}&lt;/li&gt;
          @endforeach
      &lt;/ul&gt;
      {{-- your fields here --}}
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-footer&quot;&gt;
      {{ Form::submit('Simpan', array('class'=&gt;'btn btn-primary')) }}
      &lt;button class=&quot;btn&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;Batal&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  {{ Form::close() }}

&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div id=&quot;addPenggunaModal&quot; class=&quot;modal hide fade&quot; tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; role=&quot;dialog&quot;&gt;

  {{ Form::open(array('url'=&gt;'users/addpengguna','class'=&gt;'form-horizontal','method'=&gt;'POST')) }}

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-header&quot;&gt;
      &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; class=&quot;close&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;×&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;h3&gt;Tambah Pengguna Baru&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-body&quot;&gt;
      &lt;ul&gt;
          @foreach($errors-&gt;all() as $error)
              &lt;li&gt;{{ $error }}&lt;/li&gt;
          @endforeach
      &lt;/ul&gt;
      {{-- your fields here --}}
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-footer&quot;&gt;
      {{ Form::submit('Simpan', array('class'=&gt;'btn btn-primary')) }}
      &lt;button class=&quot;btn&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;Batal&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  {{ Form::close() }}

&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>To answer your three questions:</strong></p>
<p><strong>1. Tracing POST requests</strong>: No  you don't need any browser extension. Just Open your browser's built-in DevTools (<code>F12</code>) → <strong>Network tab</strong> → submit the form → click the POST request → check the <strong>Payload</strong> tab to see what data was actually sent (or if anything was sent at all).</p>
<p><strong>2. Laravel error logs</strong>: Since inception laravel logs are always stored in <code>app/storage/logs/laravel.log</code>. Also make sure <code>'debug' =&gt; true</code> is set in <code>app/config/app.php</code> during development so errors display in the browser.</p>
<p><strong>3. Also fix your controller's failure redirect:</strong></p>
<p>php</p>
<pre><code>} else {
    return Redirect::back()-&gt;withErrors($validator)-&gt;withInput();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>Redirect::to('index')</code> on failure loses the validation errors and the user's input. <code>Redirect::back()</code> returns them to the same page with errors intact, so your <code>@foreach($errors-&gt;all())</code> will actually display something.</p>
<p><strong>Regarding your AJAX + missing token error:</strong> <code>Form::open()</code> automatically injects a hidden <code>_token</code> field for CSRF protection. If you use AJAX instead, you need to pass it manually:</p>
<pre><code>$.ajax({
    url: '/users/addpengguna',
    type: 'POST',
    data: {
        _token: $('input[name=&quot;_token&quot;]').val(),
        firstname: $('#firstname').val(),
        // ... other fields
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>what this is doing is simply grabbing the value from an hidden input named _token in your HTML DOM <code>input[name=&quot;_token&quot;]</code> and passing it onto your request /users/addpengguna laravel is wired in such a way that CSRF would work if it detects a _token field for a POST web request, otherwise it would fail, keep in mind tho that csrf_token does have an expiry time, after which your Ajax request might become stall (would not be able to send through), and you might need to refresh the page or handle that edge cases for it to work.</p>
<p>Good luck.</p>
